From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:31:00 -0700
Subject: net: airoha: ppe: queue clean-entry work with GFP_ATOMIC

queue_clean_entry_work() allocates its work record and the copied
argument with GFP_KERNEL, but the ra_sw_nat_hook_clean_entry_by_*
pointers it sits behind are exported, and wifi_lo_timeout() in
arht_wifi_lo.c calls the by_port hook from a timer callback. A
GFP_KERNEL allocation from softirq context can sleep in reclaim under
memory pressure; it goes unreported today because DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP is
off. in_atomic() cannot see a held spinlock on a PREEMPT_NONE kernel,
so a context-conditional flag is not possible; both objects are tiny.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_ppe.c
@@ -3018,7 +3018,12 @@ static int queue_clean_entry_work(enum c
 {
     struct clean_entry_work *cew;
 
-    cew = kzalloc(sizeof(*cew), GFP_KERNEL);
+    /*
+     * The ra_sw_nat_hook_clean_entry_by_* pointers are exported and one
+     * is called from a timer callback (wifi_lo_timeout), so this runs in
+     * atomic context; both objects are tiny.
+     */
+    cew = kzalloc(sizeof(*cew), GFP_ATOMIC);
     if (!cew)
         return -ENOMEM;
 
@@ -3026,7 +3031,7 @@ static int queue_clean_entry_work(enum c
     cew->type = type;
 
     if (data_len > 0) {
-        cew->data = kmemdup(data, data_len, GFP_KERNEL);
+        cew->data = kmemdup(data, data_len, GFP_ATOMIC);
         if (!cew->data) {
             kfree(cew);
             return -ENOMEM;
